語言學概論(一) Introduction to Linguistics (I)
Course Descriptions and Objectives 
This introductory-level course provides an overview of various subfields of linguistics and is designed to (1) introduce students to theoretical and structural aspects of language, as well as scientific approaches to the study of human language; (2) showcase a wide array of linguistic diversity constrained by systematic patterns and crosslinguistic universals; and (3) address the interrelationships between linguistics and other disciplines. This semester focuses on fundamental concepts and core issues in the organization of sounds (phonology/phonetics) and the form of words (morphology). English is the primary source language used to exemplify the theories and concepts surveyed in the class. Oceanic, Germanic, Latin, African languages and Mandarin are also investigated in terms of crosslinguistic variation.

Upon completion of this course, students are expected to:
•	Gain insight into the autonomy of language as an independent module; 
•	Use underlying linguistic rules to describe the forms of natural language;
•	Learn to form a hypothesis about the human language faculty and collect linguistic data to test the hypothesis; 
•	Construct the linguistic knowledge of English and other languages;
•	Apply the linguistic knowledge to other fields, such as pedagogical approaches and methods, multimedia communication, technology, and AI.

The course primarily consists of the teacher’s lectures, quizzes, written tests, two arranged exams, problem sets, vlogs, and other in-class activities. Supplementary readings and handouts are distributed in class and made available on TronClass.

